AllocTheme:
This emergency allocation will address the immediate needs as outlined below and, considering the very volatile environment, aims to be flexible in responding to the immediate and urgent needs on the ground for relocation, newly displaced persons and scale-up services in camps and overburdened host communities. Interventions will aim to scale up and complement the ongoing response, including ongoing reprogramming of existing interventions that have been affected or interrupted by the ongoing violence. This strategy has been developed based on the most recent figures and numbers and locations in the readiness and response plan and will be updated during proposals as the situation rapidly evolves.  
As such, the allocation will align with the three objectives of the humanitarian readiness and response plan:
1.	Allocation Strategic Objective (ASO) 1: Protection of civilians
	Protection services: will be implemented through an emergency response model that relies on expanding the capacity of static service points (community centers, child-friendly spaces, and women and girls’ safe spaces) and mobile outreach teams. Due to the dynamic nature of the Syrian context, this approach supports affected populations with multiple entry points to protection services and allows cluster members to continue the delivery of services in rapidly-changing circumstances to respond to existing gaps and anticipated growing needs. Procurement and provision of dignity kits are also included;
	Safe Relocation: of people to save areas by provision of transportation services. This includes relocation support and facilitation of routes for people to move safely away from hostilities and to safe destinations.
2.	Allocation Strategic Objective (ASO) 2: Provision of safe reception centers and shelters in and out of camps 
	CCCM and emergency shelter: Expansion of existing and establishment of new camps to increase emergency shelter capacity for the newly displaced IDPs, provision of camp management and coordination in the newly established and extended camps and provision of protection and mainstreamed multi-sectoral lifesaving assistance in these sites  and procurement of emergency shelter; 
	WASH: Procurement and provision of critical Water Sanitation and Hygiene (WASH) supplies and services to people in newly established and extended camp and out of camp locations  with priority to locations that are overwhelmed with new arrivals;
3.	Allocation Strategic Objective (ASO) 3: Provision of emergency services in and out of camps;
	Food assistance and livelihoods: Prepositioning and distributing of ready-to-eat-rations (RTER), food baskets, support to mobile kitchens and cooked meals to respond to the basic needs of newly displaced IDPs;  
	Health: Procurement and provision of medical supplies, including trauma and surgical kits/supplies, anesthetics drugs and supplementary medicines, support to ambulance services, mobile clinics in IDPs sites and facilities in targeted locations that are overwhelmed with new arrivals;
	Non-Food Items (NFIs) and emergency shelter: Procurement and provision of core pipelines (Non-Food Items (NFIs) and shelter materials that are considered indispensable for the delivery of the frontline response; 
	Common Services: increased logistic support to humanitarian partners through coordinating transshipment services and delivery of humanitarian cargo from Turkey into northwest Syria via the Bab Al-Hawa and Bab Al-Salam border crossings; 
	Nutrition: scale-up of nutrition services to respond to increased needs where acute malnutrition of children 6-59 months has almost double one out of each three mothers and children in NWS is acutely malnourished and /or potentially anemic.
